supervising each binary under its own scratch launchd agent on :8997

  reference: yes,yes,yes,-e
  swift:     yes,yes,yes,-e
  (started, relaunched-after-SIGKILL, stayed-down-after-bootout, log-streams-written)
  the fourth term is which streams carry bytes: o=stdout, e=stderr, - =empty, none=silent.
  Both routers log to stderr only, so `-e` is the agreeing answer, not a partial one.

  ok   both binaries survive the installer's supervision identically

supervising each binary's WATCH agent — RunAtLoad + WatchPaths, no KeepAlive

  reference: yes,yes,yes,--
  swift:     yes,yes,yes,--
  (ran-at-load, re-ran-on-file-change, one-shot-not-resident, log-streams-written)
  launchd's own accounting: ts:runs=1->2:stages=1 swift:runs=1->2:stages=1 
  `stages` is how many times the watched file had to be changed before launchd delivered an
  event. More than 1 is launchd coalescing or dropping a delivery, not a router misbehaving.

  note both binaries answered the watch agent's supervision identically this run
       (yes,yes,yes,--), and launchd's own counter is the witness for the one-shot term.
       The row stays BLOCKED and NOTHING is recorded: the `reran` term reads yes about
       one run in three even when no stimulus can reach the watched path (4 of 6 decoy
       trials correct, 2 of 6 spurious), so agreement on it is not evidence of parity.

